what was in the mind of winston  churchill why did he think  that he could afford not to negotiate  in terms of supreme command the allies  were very lucky  because had the war  broken out when stanley baldwin was  prime minister i think britain would  have not done very well at all  it broke out when naval neville  chamberlain was prime minister  but quickly he began to bring people in  the cabinet  who advised him corrected him  educated him about what was going on  he still felt that there was some chance  of a negotiated  peace after the fall of france but on  may 10  1940 the day that germany invaded france  there was a radical change of government  winston churchill came in and  churchill was determined to not  surrender not negotiate  but not only fight the germans and the  italians  but defeat them now this was very  strange idea because  within three weeks there would just be  britain  and there would be all of occupied  europe so what was in the mind of  winston churchill why did he think  that he could afford not to negotiate  hitler had offered him a deal where he  said we will not invade or bomb you  and we will let you have your empire  just call off the war  and then hitler thought that he would  turn his attention then without a worry  over  either britain or the united states and  he could turn his attention  to the soviet union but churchill was  not just a romantic  he was a very brilliant guy who was  convinced  that the last minute armament of the  royal air force  given the fact and given the fact that  the royal navy was the largest navy in  the world meant  everybody took a deep breath they said  to themselves  for all of german's continental ground  power  land forces they cannot get across the  channel because  the british navy will sink it sink ships  from the german navy  and they do not have the ability to  achieve error  superiority over britain and therefore  they can't attack us  and churchill said well you know what we  we can't invade either  so there's going to be a standoff and  that standoff is going to favor  us because eventually authoritarians  like hitler  and stalin cannot trust each other  they'll have a falling out  and at some point they will fight each  other and that will be to  our advantage and at some point we  understand that either the japanese or  the germans or the italians will do  something stupid  and draw the united states into the war  churchill was already envisioning an  alliance that was to happen  a year and a half later of the soviet  union  the united states and the british empire  against the axis triad and they would  have enormous advantages in terms of  manpower population industrial strength  and uh geography and that's exactly what  happened  in america it's hard to think how we  would have been prepared  for pearl harbor without franklin  roosevelt by that i mean  the republican party was primarily  isolationist  and the democratic party was focused  on the great depression and so at this  time people were saying we cannot  invest in military expenses and armies  and navies and air forces  fdr along with a brilliant congressman  carl vinson  thought otherwise and fdr understood  that  wherever we were going to fight pacific  or european theaters we needed a navy to  transport  soldiers over there and to protect them  from coming over here  so in a series of naval acts in the late  1930s the carl vinson  naval act one two three and etc  we had on the books a  new pacific fleet about five times  larger  than was at pearl harbor and much better  characterized  by baltimore heavy cruisers essex  carriers  the iowa class battleships that was  already on  the books it was already budgeted before  we went in  to world war ii and fdr and sometimes kg  machiavellian fashion  was reassuring an isolationist nation in  1940 that it would not go to war  even though he was taking quite dramatic  steps to prepare it to go to war and  might even welcome  it going to war at the second world war  was ultimately a story of people  how people died and how people made  decisions that determine which side won  or lost  i think we can sum it up by the results  because the proof of the pudding  is always in the eating and when we look  at this vast  canvas of death that was the second  world war 65 million people  we have to ask ourselves why were they  able to be so successful in the war  and i think the answer is the united  states and  the british had an overarching principle  that they were going to find  ways to win the war and to wage it  with the least possible uh  injury to their own country's  populations and that was completely  diametrically opposed  to the axis powers war of aggression  where they thought that  we're super people we're going to just  destroy inferiors and if we lose people  it's a glorious thing to die during a  war or in the soviet union's idea that  all that matters is survival of the  soviet state millions do not matter if  the soviet principle the soviet project  survives  ultimately then the story of people  turns out to be  the brilliance of winston churchill and  franklin roosevelt  and leading the democracies not just to  war but to  a victorious war and  at very little cost in comparison to the  other belligerents  thank you for watching we hope you're  enjoying our highlight series and invite  you to explore our complete course  on the second world wars all of  hillsdale college's online courses are  free  and for everyone who loves to learn
 

